module

public import Sheaf.Algebra.Lie.KostantIntegralForm
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Lie.Semisimple.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Lie.Weights.Basic
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.RootSystem.Base
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.RootSystem.BaseExists

/-!
# Integral triangular PBW for the Kostant form

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 23h, pp. 508–510, the integral-enveloping-algebra
input to admissible lattices before equation (152); Kostant's integral PBW theorem
(blueprint: lem-sfb-kostant-integral-pbw).

The root pairing uses the evaluation pairing on the splitting abelian subalgebra. All
Chevalley-basis conditions are explicit. Both simple roots and positive roots have arbitrary
finite enumerations; neither a nonempty root system nor a nonzero Lie algebra is required.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

namespace LieAlgebra

/-- Ordered negative divided powers, simple-coroot binomials, and positive divided powers
form an integer basis of the subalgebra generated by all root divided powers. Representations
extend uniquely along the canonical generator map, and the zero Lie algebra gives `ℤ`.
Source: the Kostant integral PBW input to Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 23h,
pp. 508–510, before equation (152) (blueprint: lem-sfb-kostant-integral-pbw).

Proof plan:
1. Use `lem-sfb-root-base-rational-integral-spans` to express every coroot as an integral
   combination of simple coroots and every root-coroot pairing as an integer. Apply
   `lem-sfb-integral-newton-expansion` to shifted coroot binomials and products of toral
   binomials. The commuting toral elements allow evaluation of these polynomial identities.
2. Prove membership of each simple-coroot binomial by induction on its degree. In
   `lem-sfb-sl2-divided-straightening` with `m = n = b`, the last term is `binom(h_i,b)`.
   All earlier toral factors have smaller degree and integral Newton expansions. Subtract
   those terms from the product of the two generating root divided powers.
3. Swap distinct nonopposite root blocks by `lem-sfb-chevalley-rank-two-straightening`.
   Opposite roots use `lem-sfb-sl2-divided-straightening`; its non-leading terms have strictly
   smaller total degree after Newton expansion. A toral/root swap shifts the toral variable
   by an integer root pairing; its correction again has smaller degree and integral expansion.
4. Induct on total degree and then on inversions in the chosen triangular type order.
   Each leading swap decreases inversions, while every correction decreases total degree.
   Merge equal root blocks by the integral binomial multiplication formula and equal toral
   blocks by Step 1. Thus every word in the generating divided powers is an integer linear
   combination of the displayed monomials. Step 2 gives the converse membership.
5. Apply `lem-sfb-rational-pbw` to the ordered Chevalley basis. Each displayed monomial has
   the corresponding ordinary PBW monomial as leading term with nonzero reciprocal-factorial
   coefficient. In a finite relation, comparison in the largest total degree and descending
   induction prove rational, hence integer, linear independence. Together with Step 4 this
   constructs the claimed basis.
6. Apply `lem-sfb-enveloping-universal-property` with `A = Module.End ℚ V` and the representing
   Lie homomorphism to obtain the unique extension and its values on generators. For the
   zero Lie algebra rational PBW leaves just the monomial one, and the generated unital
   integer subalgebra consists exactly of integer multiples of one. -/
theorem exists_kostant_integral_pbw_basis
    (L : Type u) [LieRing L] [LieAlgebra ℚ L] [FiniteDimensional ℚ L]
    [HasTrivialRadical ℚ L] (H : LieSubalgebra ℚ L) [IsLieAbelian H]
    (hmax : ∀ K : LieSubalgebra ℚ L, H ≤ K → IsLieAbelian K → K = H)
    (I : Type v) [Finite I] (P : RootPairing I ℚ (Module.Dual ℚ H) H)
    [P.IsRootSystem] [P.IsCrystallographic] [P.IsReduced]
    (heval : ∀ (a : Module.Dual ℚ H) (h : H), P.toLinearMap a h = a h)
    (Δ : P.Base) (r N : ℕ) (δ : Fin r ≃ Δ.support)
    (β : Fin N ≃ {i : I // Δ.IsPos i}) (e : I → L)
    (he : ∀ i : I, e i ∈ LieModule.weightSpace L (P.root i) ∧ e i ≠ 0)
    (hopp : ∀ i : I, ⁅e i, e (P.reflectionPerm i i)⁆ = (P.coroot i : L))
    (hadd : ∀ i j k : I, P.root i + P.root j = P.root k →
      ∃ ε : ℤ, (ε = 1 ∨ ε = -1) ∧
        ⁅e i, e j⁆ = ((ε : ℚ) * (P.chainBotCoeff i j + 1)) • e k)
    (hzero : ∀ i j : I, P.root i + P.root j ∉ Set.range P.root →
      P.root i + P.root j ≠ 0 → ⁅e i, e j⁆ = 0)
    (B : Module.Basis (Fin r ⊕ I) ℚ L)
    (Bh : ∀ i : Fin r, B (Sum.inl i) = (P.coroot (δ i) : L))
    (Be : ∀ i : I, B (Sum.inr i) = e i) :
    (∀ d : (Fin N → ℕ) × (Fin r → ℕ) × (Fin N → ℕ),
      kostantPBWMonomial (fun i ↦ (P.coroot (δ i) : L))
        (fun j ↦ e (P.reflectionPerm (β j).val (β j).val))
        (fun j ↦ e (β j).val) d ∈ kostantIntegralForm e) ∧
    (∃ b : Module.Basis ((Fin N → ℕ) × (Fin r → ℕ) × (Fin N → ℕ)) ℤ
        (kostantIntegralForm e),
      ∀ d, (b d : UniversalEnvelopingAlgebra ℚ L) =
        kostantPBWMonomial (fun i ↦ (P.coroot (δ i) : L))
          (fun j ↦ e (P.reflectionPerm (β j).val (β j).val))
          (fun j ↦ e (β j).val) d) ∧
    (∀ (V : Type w) [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℚ V] [LieRingModule L V] [LieModule ℚ L V],
      ∃! φ : UniversalEnvelopingAlgebra ℚ L →ₐ[ℚ] Module.End ℚ V,
        ∀ x : L, φ (UniversalEnvelopingAlgebra.ι ℚ x) = LieModule.toEnd ℚ L V x) ∧
    (Subsingleton L → Function.Bijective (algebraMap ℤ (kostantIntegralForm e))) := by
  sorry

end LieAlgebra
